## 1. Introduction
Supercapacitors (SCs), a category of important energy storage devices, have attracted increasing attention due to the excellent performances, such as rapid charge–discharge rates, stable cycling capability, and high power density [1–3]. However, SCs have limited applications because they have exhibited lower energy densities than conventional batteries [4,5]. Improving the energy density of SCs without sacrificing their power capability is a pressing issue encountered in fulfilling the growing demand for energy. An intriguing electrode architecture is a crucial factor in manufacturing high-performance Faraday pseudocapacitors for actual application in SCs [6,7]. Thus far, the use of various nanomaterials, such as C materials [8], metal oxide/hydroxides [7,9], sulfides [10,11], phosphides [12], nitrides [13], conductive polymers [14], and their composites [15–17], as SC electrode materials has been exploxed. However, the above-mentioned materials, which possess various nanostructures, have inevitable shortcomings, such as tedious preparation procedures, small capacitance, and poor stability [18]. In this regard, it is highly desirable to explore the feasible and efficient strategies for developing the superior SC electrode materials.
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs), a class of three-dimensional (3D) hybrid materials constructed on the basis of metal cations and organic ligands, have shown great potential applications in chemical sensors, catalysis, batteries, and SCs due to their controllable structures and versatile functionalities [19]. Theoretically, MOFbased materials are promising for electrochemical energy storage due to their merits of large specific surface area, high porosity, and variable sites for redox reactions [20]. However, pristine MOFs often show several critical drawbacks, including low conductivity and particle aggregation [21]. These disadvantages remarkably limit their applications as electrode materials. Considerable effort has been devoted to the solution of the above shortcomings. The high-temperature annealing of MOFs is the most commonly used method among various modification strategies. This technique can produce various hybrid materials that contain metals, metal oxides, and metal sulfur/phosphorus/tin compounds [22,23]. For example, $\mathsf{C o S e_{2}/C N T}$ [4], $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{4}$ [24], NiCoSe [25], $\mathsf{N i}_{\mathsf{x}}\mathsf{C o}_{3-\mathsf{x}}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ [26], and $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped C sponge composites [27] have been fabricated from diverse MOFs and utilized as electrode materials for SCs [28]. Nonetheless, the fabrication procedures of these MOF-derived materials often involve complicated pyrolysis processes with high energy consumption [29]. Moreover, these MOF derivatives also display either relatively low capacitance or limited cycling capacity, mainly due to the reduction in exposed active sites caused by the shrinkage of MOF networks or the agglomeration of formed metal particles during calcination. The integration of MOFs with electrochemically active materials, such as metal oxides [30], conducting polymers [31,32], and various C-based materials [33], can yield the heterostructured MOFs, which can efficiently merge the merits of MOFs, e.g., large surface area and ordered pores, with those of nanomaterials, e.g., high electrical conductivity and dense active sites [34]. Among the diverse C-related nanomaterials, onedimensional (1D) carbon nanotubes (CNTs) are of particular interest due to their high surface area, good mechanical strength, and considerable electrical conductivity [35]. CNTs are combined with MOFs to improve the electrochemical performance of MOFs [36– 38]. However, most MOFs are only physically integrated with CNTs, given that CNTs prefer to aggregate together due to high surface energy; this phenomenon usually results in unsatisfactory SC performances [39,40]. Considering this situation, the in situ growth of MOF layers around CNTs with large internal diameters, uniform wall thicknesses, and high specific surface areas is believed to be a promising way to generate diverse heterostructures that can fully and effectively combine the advantages of MOFs and CNTs [41].
Two-dimensional (2D) MOF nanosheets (NSs) are promising candidate materials for energy storage that can efficiently merge the features of MOFs and ultrathin 2D nanomaterials [42–45]. 2D MOF NSs have larger exposed catalytic active surfaces and more accessible active sites than their bulk counterparts [46], and thus present extensive applications in clean energy and energy storage [47], especially in SCs [48]. Among numerous MOFs available, zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) consisting of transition metal ions and imidazole-based ligands have been developed due to their several unique features and superior performance in various applications [49]. In particular, various ZIF-derived composites have been utilized as superior SCs owing to their intrinsically high surface areas and convenient preparation processes [50–52]. However, the direct use of 2D ZIF NSs as SC electrode materials has been rarely reported thus far. 1D/2D heterojunctions that are composed of 1D nanowires/nanotubes and 2D ultra-thin NSs have great potential applications in photoelectrochemical cells [53], photocatalysis [54], and energy storage [55]. In these structures, 1D nanowires/nanotubes enable quick charge transfer along axes, and 2D NSs confine electrons in their unique atomic layers [56,57]. The heterostructure construction strategy can induce a strong synergistic effect that considerably enhances performance [58]. Considering the merits of 2D ZIF NSs and CNTs, the development and use of novel 1D/2D ZIF@CNT heterostructures as superior SC electrodes are highly desirable.
In this work, we aim to develop a series of 1D/2D ZIF@CNT heterostructure hybrids for SCs through the in situ growth of bimetallic CoNi-ZIF NSs around $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped CNTs (N-CNTs) (Fig. 1a). These hybrids can efficiently combine the advantages of their two components. The $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -CNTs were prepared by annealing glucose and the melamine-cyanuric acid complex at $900^{\circ}\mathsf C$ under ${\sf N}_{2}$ atmosphere. As a result of $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking interaction, ultrathin CoNi-ZIF NSs grew around the CNT surface, thus yielding the 1D/2D ZIF@CNTs heterostructures. Given that the CoNi-ZIF NSs comprise mixed metal nodes and exhibit a high specific surface area, an ultrathin NS layer, and abundant active sites, the SC capabilities of the series of ZIF@CNTs heterostructures can be precisely regulated on the basis of the dosage of the metal ions (Co, Ni) present in CoNi-ZIFs [59]. When the molar ratio of Co ions to Ni ions is 1:1, the CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2 presents a superior specific capacitance of $1118\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ at $1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ and rate performance due to its unique heterostructure and the synergistic effect of its multiple components. Moreover, the assembled hybrid device based on CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2 demonstrates great potential for application in SCs. The proposed CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT electrode has three substantial advantages over other MOF-based SC systems: (i) feasible and convenient synthesis strategy without calcination or other treatments; (ii) the controllable 1D/2D heterogeneous structures obtained via in situ synthetic route promote charge transfer at the interface; and (iii) superior electrochemical capacitive performance and high energy density endowed by the strong synergistic effect among N-CNTs, CoNi-ZIF NSs, CoO, and $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ phases. This work offers a general approach for the construction of MOF composites with advanced performance for electrical energy storage device.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Chemicals and reagents
Melamine, cyanuric acid, cobaltous nitrate $(\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}.6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O};$ and nickel nitrate $(\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})$ were purchased from Aladdin Chemical Reagent Co. Ltd. (Shanghai, China). Dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO), 2-methylimidazole $(\mathsf{C}_{4}\mathsf{H}_{6}\mathsf{N}_{2})$ and glucose were purchased from Solarbio Life Sciences Reagent Co. Ltd. (Beijing, China). Anhydrous methanol $(\mathrm{CH}_{3}0\mathrm{H})$ was purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). All of the chemicals used were of analytical reagent grade and used without further purification. Milli-Q water $(>18.2\ \Omega\ \mathrm{cm})$ was used throughout this study.
## 2.2. Synthesis of N-CNTs
## 2.3. Synthesis of CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT
Typically, $50~\mathrm{mg}~N$ -CNTs were dispersed in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol $(\mathrm{CH}_{3}0\mathrm{H})$ , following by pouring $_{0.5\mathrm{~g~}}$ of $C_{0}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and $_{0.5\mathrm{~g~}}$ of ${\mathsf{N i}}({\mathsf{N O}}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathsf{H}}_{2}0$ and sonicating for $15\ \mathrm{min}$ (solution A1). The mixed solution was prepared without $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -CNT and represented as solution A2. 2-methylimidazole ( $C_{4}\mathrm{H}_{6}\mathrm{N}_{2}$ , $\mathbf{1.32\g}$ was added in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol and sonicated for $15\mathrm{min}$ , obtained the solution B. Subsequently, the solution B was slowly added dropwise to the solution A1 under stirring. After finishing the addition, the mixture was further stirred for $60\ \mathrm{min}$ . Then, the resultant solution was washed 3–5 times with anhydrous methanol, following centrifuged with the rotation speed of $10,000\mathrm{~r~min}^{-1}$ for $3\ \mathrm{min}^{\cdot}$ ). The obtained intermediate product was dissolved in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of anhydrous methanol (denoted as solution C). Afterward, the solution A2 was slowly added dropwise to the solution C under stirring and stirred for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ . The thoroughly mixed solution was transferred to a $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ autoclave and kept at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ in a forced air oven. Finally, the resulting was centrifuged with anhydrous methanol. The product was denoted as CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2. For compared, two composites of CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-1 and CoNiZIF@N-CNT-3 were prepared using the similar approach, but using the mass ratios of $C_{0}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and ${\sf N i}({\sf N O}_{3})_{2}.6{\sf H}_{2}0$ of 3:1 and 1:3, respectively, while the total mass of the two metal salts was ${\textsc{1g}}.$ . Also, the pristine CoNi-ZIF NSs were prepared without the addition of $N\mathrm{.}$ -CNTs.
## 2.4. Electrochemical measurements
A series of CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT composites as well as N-CNTs and CoNi-ZIF NSs were acted as the working electrodes. $80~\mathrm{wt\%}$ CoNiZIF@N-CNT, $10\mathrm{\Deltawt\%}$ carbon black, and $10\mathrm{wt\%}$ polyvinylidene fluoride were mixed and dispersed in $200~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $N$ -methyl-2- pyrrolidone. The gained homogeneous slurry was coated onto the surface of Ni foam, thus serving as the working electrode. The manufactured working electrode was dried at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ in vacuum. Concurrently, $\mathrm{Hg/HgO}$ and Pt plate were used as the reference electrode and the counter electrode, respectively. Electrochemical measurements were performed in $6.0\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH on electrochemical workstation $\mathrm{'CHI660E)}$ in a three-electrode cell at ambient temperature. Moreover, galvanostatic charge–discharge (GCD) tests were performed using a CT2001A LAND Cell test system. The specific capacitance $(C,{\mathsf{F}}{\mathsf{g}}^{-1})$ was measured based on tested GCD curves according to Eq (1):
$$
C=\frac{I\times\Delta t}{m\times\Delta V}
$$
where the $I,\mathbf{m},\Delta t$ , and $\Delta V$ represent the discharge current (A), the mass of active material (g), discharge time (s), and potential drop (V), respectively.
In addition, the asymmetric SC was fabricated with CoNiZIF@N-CNT-2 and activated carbon as the positive and negative electrodes, respectively. The mass ratio of the CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2 to activated carbon was calculated based on Eq (2):
$$
\frac{\mathfrak{m}_{+}}{\mathfrak{m}_{-}}=\frac{C_{-}\times\Delta V_{-}}{C_{+}\times\Delta V_{+}}
$$
where m (mg) denotes the mass of electrode materials, $C(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{F}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1})$ is specific capacitance, and $\Delta V$ (V) represents potential window. The energy density (E, Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ ) and power density $(P,\mathsf{W}\mathrm{kg}^{-1})$ of the device were determined according to Eq (3) and Eq (4), respectively.
$$
\begin{array}{l}{E=\displaystyle\frac{0.5C\times(\Delta V)^{2}}{3.6}}\\ {P=\displaystyle\frac{E\times3600}{\Delta t}}\end{array}
$$
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Surface morphology and chemical structure of CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT composites
Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR), Raman spectroscopy and $\mathsf{x}$ -ray diffraction (XRD) were performed to characterize the chemical structures of the CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT composites. The absorption peaks in the FT-IR spectra (Fig. S5a) are assigned to the combination of $N$ -CNTs and CoNi-ZIF NSs. The peak at approximately $3453~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ represents the $0{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ functional group, [66] and that at approximately $1641~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is ascribed to $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ vibration, which originated from the graphitic domains of $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -CNTs and CoNi-ZIF NSs [67]. The peaks at approximately 1385 and $1065~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are assigned to the $\mathsf{C}{\mathsf{-}}0$ stretching vibration and umbrella vibration of $-\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ from CoNi-ZIF NSs [67], respectively. The two main peaks found at 1350 and $1593~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in the Raman spectra of CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT (Fig. S5b) are due to D and G bands, which represent the $s p^{3}$ -type disordered C and $\scriptstyle s p^{2}$ -type ordered graphitic C from N-CNTs, respectively [68]. The relative intensity ratio of the D-band to the G-band $(I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}})$ is indicative of the disorders and structural defects in the C skeleton [69]. The $I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}$ values of CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-1, CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2, and CoNi-ZIF $@N$ -CNT-3 are 0.99, 1.07, 1.02, respectively, and are slightly larger than those of N-CNTs (0.97). These results demonstrate that the in-situ growth of ZIF NSs on $N$ -CNTs can increase the defect degree of composites, and the existence of additional defects in CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2 is believed to favor fast charge transportation and energy storage in SC application. Consistent with those of CoNi-ZIFs, the XRD patterns of diverse CoNi-ZIF $@N$ -CNT hybrids (Fig. S5c) exhibit four diffraction peaks at approximately $10.1^{\circ}$ , $20.3^{\circ},~34.1^{\circ}$ and $60.3^{\circ}$ [70]. These findings indicate that CoNi-ZIF NSs have fully covered $N$ -CNTs. The nitrogen adsorption/desorption curves were acquired to evaluate the surface areas of all the samples. Fig. S5d depicts that the series of CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT composites exhibit type IV isotherms with a significant hysteresis loop that is indicative of their mesoporous nature. The specific surface areas of CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT1, CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2, and CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-3 are estimated to be 304.12, 217.01, and $232.69\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. Among the tested materials, CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2 has the smallest specific surface area. Interestingly, the specific surface areas of the series of CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT are obviously smaller than those of $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -CNTs (721.1 $\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , Fig. S1f) but larger than those of CoNi-ZIF NSs $\cdot116.56~\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , Fig. S2f). These results indicate that the $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -CNTs that are dominated by microporous structures are conducive to the in situ growth of CoNi-ZIF NSs on N-CNTs. These structures can effectively improve the agglomeration of ZIF NSs and increase specific surface area. High large specific surface area may promote ion transfer at the electrode/electrolyte interface, and the synergistic effects caused by the effective combination of N-CNTs and CoNiZIF NSs can enhance the electrochemical performance of the composite.
## 3.2. Electrochemical performances of a series of CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT composites
The electrochemical performances of a series of CoNi-ZIF@NCNT hybrids were evaluated in $6.0\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH by using a threeelectrode system. For comparison, the $N.$ -CNT and CoNi-ZIF NS electrodes were tested under the same conditions. Fig. 3a shows the typical CV curves of $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -CNT, CoNi-ZIF NS and the series of CoNiZIF@N-CNT electrodes in a potential window range of $_{0-0.6\mathrm{~V~}}$ (vs. $\mathsf{H g}/\mathsf{H g}0)$ at a scan rate of $5\mathrm{m}\mathrm{v}s^{-1}$ . The CoNi-ZIF NS electrode exhibits distinct redox peaks at the potential of 0.13 and $0.29{\mathrm{~V~}}$ that are attributable to the valence change between ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{I I}})$ and Co (III) [75]. Weak redox peaks at the potentials of 0.23 and $_{0.38\mathrm{~V~}}$ have also appeared owing to the valence change between Ni(II) and Ni(III) [69]. The two pairs of redox peaks of $\mathrm{Co(II)/Co(III)}$ and $\mathsf{N i}(\mathrm{II})/\mathsf{N i}(\mathrm{III})$ are found for the CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-1 and CoNiZIF@N-CNT-2 electrodes. Comparison revealed that the peak current densities of the ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}/{\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ and $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}/\mathsf{N i}^{3+}$ of the CoNi-ZIF@NCNT-2 electrode are higher than those of CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-1, hinting a superior electrochemical activity. However, only the redox peaks of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}/\mathsf{N i}^{3+}$ are present in the CV curve of the CoNi-ZIF@NCNT-3 electrode likely because the composite contains large amounts of Ni ions. Notably, the $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -CNT electrode shows only an insignificant redox peak with low current density, revealing its negligible contribution to the high CV response of the CoNiZIF@N-CNT electrodes. This result verifies that the large CV curve mainly originated from the CoNi-ZIF component contained by the composites. The redox peaks can be ascribed to the reversible faradaic redox reactions of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}/{\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ and $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}/\mathsf{N i}^{3+}$ in the presence of $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ [76,77], as described in the following equations:
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{\mathbf{C0^{2+}}+\mathbf{\Phi}\mathbf{0H^{-}}\hookrightarrow\mathbf{C0^{2+}}\left(\mathbf{0H}\right)+\mathbf{e}^{-1}}\\ &{}\\ &{\mathbf{Ni^{2+}}+\mathbf{\Phi}\mathbf{0H^{-}}\hookrightarrow\mathbf{Ni^{2+}}\left(\mathbf{0H}\right)+\mathbf{e}^{-1}}\\ &{}\\ &{\mathbf{C0^{2+}}\left(\mathbf{0H}\right)\hookrightarrow\mathbf{C0^{3+}}\left(\mathbf{0H}\right)+\mathbf{e}^{-1}}\\ &{}\\ &{\mathbf{Ni^{2+}}\left(\mathbf{0H}\right)\hookrightarrow\mathbf{Ni^{3+}}\left(\mathbf{0H}\right)+\mathbf{e}^{-1}}\end{array}
$$
The specific capacitances of the series of CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT hybrids were further studied on the basis of GCD measurements that were acquired at a constant current density of 1 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ (Fig. 3b). All of the CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT composites and CoNi-ZIF NSs show excellent symmetric GCD curves that are indicative of the occurrence of redox reactions and are line with the CV results. Compared with N-CNTs, CoNi-ZIF NSs, CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-1, and CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-3 electrodes, the CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2 electrode displays a longer discharge time, indicating larger specific capacitance. As a result, the CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2 electrode exhibits the largest specific capacitance of $1118\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ in accordance with Eq. (1). The remarkably larger capacitance of the CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2 electrode than those of $N\mathrm{.}$ -CNTs $(80.4\mathrm{F}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ), CoNi-ZIF NSs $(834\mathrm{F}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ), CoNi-ZIF $@N.$ -CNT-1 $\left\langle499\mathrm{~F~g~}^{-1}\right\rangle$ and CoNi-ZIF $@N$ -CNT-3 (876.3 F $\mathbf{g}^{-1}.$ ) (Fig. 3c) illustrates that the integration of CoNi-ZIF NSs with $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -CNTs can considerably enhance electrochemical activity and capacitor performance. As mentioned above, CoNi-ZIF $@N.$ -CNT-2 and CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-3 comprise the $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ phase, which is conducive to the great improvement in the specific capacitance of electrode materials [24]. The CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-1 electrode lacks a clear $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ phase can be observed shows only a small specific capacitance. The SC performance of the present CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT2 electrode is superior to those of MOF-related SCs (Table S2). The superior electrochemical performance of the CoNi-ZIF@NCNT-2 electrode can be attributed to its unique 1D/2D heterostructure and the synergistic effect between the two components. This fluffy heterostructure is favorable for the rapid diffusion of electrolytes and thus provides a highly electroactive network for accelerating ion migration [71,75].
CV curves and GCD tests were performed at different scan rates and current densities to examine further electrochemical performances of the developed electrode materials. The CV curves of the CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2 electrode were acquired at various scan rates of 2 to $50\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ (Fig. 3d). The results illustrate that each CV curve demonstrates similar shapes at different scan rates, revealing fast and reversible redox reactions [78]. In addition, CV curves with similar appearances and consistent shapes are obtained for the diverse electrodes of $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -CNTs, CoNi-ZIF NSs, CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-1, and CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-3 at the scan rates of
$2{\-}50\ \mathrm{\mV}\ \mathsf{s}^{-1}$ (Fig. S7). Furthermore, the analogous shapes of the GCD curves obtained at different applied current densities of $1-10\mathrm{~A~g}^{-1}$ (Fig. 3e) with the increase in current density suggest good pseudocapacitive preference and advantageous faradaic reversibility. Moreover, the specific capacitance of the CoNiZIF@N-CNT-2 electrode decreases from $1118\mathrm{~F~}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ to 960.4 F $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ as the current density is increased over the range of 1–10 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . Therefore, this electrode exhibits excellent rate capability with $85.9\%$ capacity retention. The reduction in specific capacitance at high current density is mainly due to the restriction of ion transfer rate at high current density [25]. The shapes of GCD curves of $N$ -CNTs, CoNi-ZIF NSs, CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-1 and CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-3 at different current densities (Fig. S8) also do not demonstrate substantial changes. These findings are indicative of faradaic pseudocapacity and double-layer capacity at different potential ranges and are due to the redox reaction of Co and Ni species, whereas the double-layer capacitance is a result of the synergistic effect between N-CNTs and CoNi-ZIF NSs. Moreover, the $N\mathrm{.}$ -CNTs, CoNi-ZIF, CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-1, and CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-3 electrodes present retention rates of $56.9\%$ $78.0\%$ , $79.5\%$ , and $88.3\%$ at the high current density of $10\mathrm{~A~}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . The corresponding specific capacitance values of the five electrodes at different current densities are summarized in Fig. 3f and Table S3. Clearly, these results verify that the specific capacitance of the CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT electrode can be feasibly regulated by the usage of a metal precursor in the preparation of the composite.
The electrochemical impedance spectrometry (EIS) Nquist plots of all the samples were recorded to determine the change in their conductivity (Fig. S9). Among the diverse electrodes, the $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -CNTs possess the lowest charge transfer resistance $\left(R_{c t}\right)$ of $0.34\Omega$ , which is suggestive of their superior electrochemical performance that stem from the extraordinary electrical structure and large surface area of CNTs [79]. Moreover, the CoNi-ZIF NS electrode exhibits a $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ value of $1.01\Omega$ . Among the three CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT composites, CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2 shows the smallest radius of the semicircle in the high-frequency region, and a high slope of the line in the low frequency region, and the smallest $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ value of $0.52\Omega$ These findings verify that CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2 exhibits superior electric conductivity at the electrode/electrolyte interface [80]. The remarkable specific capacitance of CoNi-ZIF $@N.$ -CNT-2 can be ascribed to the following aspects: (i) N-CNTs with high conductivity and large surface area can not only act as a highway for charge transfer but can also efficiently serve as the scaffold for the growth of CoNi-ZIF NSs; this behavior results in the homogeneous dispersion of NSs and the exposure additional active sites for electrochemical reactions [81]. (ii) The porous structure and framework topology of CoNi-ZIF NSs provide channels for rapid electron transfer and disperse the stress generated in electrochemical reactions [51]. Furthermore, due to the synergistic effect of its two components, the 1D/2D heterostructure of CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT benefits charge transfer such that the electrochemical performances are promoted. (iii) The embedded $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ and CoO phases behave as effective promoters to improve electronic transfer between the conductive skeleton and the active species [24]. All these characteristics are responsible for the excellent electrochemical performance of CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2.
Given that the CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2 electrode exhibits superior specific capacitance, it can be used as an excellent SC material. An asymmetric SC (ASC) was constructed to investigate the practical application of CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2 when used as an electrode material for electrocapacitor. CV tests on CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2 and commercial AC electrodes were carried out at $5\mathrm{m}\mathrm{V}\thinspace s^{-1}$ to investigate the appropriate optimal voltage window of the ASC (Fig. S10). Clearly, the CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2 and AC electrodes can well work within the potential ranges of $_{0-0.6\mathrm{V}}$ and $-1.0$ to $_{0\vee}$ , respectively.
Fig. 4a shows the CV curves of the CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2//AC ASC at different potential windows in $6.0\mathrm{M}$ KOH solution. The anodic current density only shows an insignificant increase with the increment in potential even within the potential window of $_{0-1.7\mathrm{~V~}}$ . Polarization occurs at $1.8{\ V}_{\ }$ Therefore, the working voltage of the CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2//AC ASC was set at $1.7~\mathrm{V}$ . The GCD curves of the CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2//AC at $2\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ were also acquired at different cell voltages (Fig. 4b). The results show that consistent with the CV curves, the charge–discharge curves present unchanged shapes over $_{0-1.7\mathrm{V}}$ . On the basis of GCD curves, the specific capacitances of the CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2//AC at cell voltage of 0–1.3, 0–1.4, 0–1.5, 0–1.6, 0–1.7 V were calculated as 82.2, 98.0, 111.6, 117.3, and $122.2\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ , respectively (Fig. 4c). The obvious increase in specific capacitance from $82.2\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ to $122.2\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ indicates enhanced energy storage in the ASC. The electrochemical properties of the CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2//AC ASC were further examined over the range of $_{0-1.7\mathrm{V}}$ in $6.0\mathrm{M}$ KOH. Fig. 4d presents the CV curves that were measured at different scan rates from $5\mathrm{m}\mathrm{V}s^{-1}$ to $50\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ . The shapes of the CV curves remain the same with the increase in scan rate. Therefore, the manufactured ASC device exhibits superior rate capability performance. Moreover, GCD curves were recorded at different current densities (Fig. 4e). The symmetric shape of the GCD curves indicates capacitor-like behavior. The specific capacitances of the ASC devices that were calculated on the basis of Eq. (1) are summarized in Fig. 4f and Table S4. The specific capacitances of CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2 //AC is 127.4 and $80\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ at 1 and $10\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, indicating good rate performance. The gradual decrement in specific capacitance with the increase of current density is mainly due to the low utilization efficiency of CoNi-ZIF $@$ N- CNT-2//AC under high charge and discharge current density [82].
The electrochemical impedance of the CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2//AC ASC was also investigated. Fig. ${\bf49}$ depicts the EIS Nyquist plot and corresponding equivalent circuit. A semi-circle with small diameter is observed in the high-frequency region, yielding a small $R_{\mathrm{ct}}\left(2.3\Omega\right)$ for the developed ASC device. This result suggests that the as-developed device possesses excellent electron conductivity and fast charge-transfer capability. Moreover, charge/discharge cycle performance was examined at the constant current density of $5\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Fig. 4h). The capacitance retention after 10,000 cycles is $72.55\%$ . This result illustrates the good cycling stability of the ASC. The large capacity loss in the initial stage could be due to the imperfection of the coating process and the shedding of a large amount of electrode material during the long-cycle test [59]. Moreover, considering that the CoNi-ZIF component in the as-prepared CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2//AC ASC is a skeleton organic material that does not undergo calcination, the capacitance loss maybe due to the degradation and morphological change of CoNi-ZIF NSs during the whole cycle [83]. The nanostructure and chemical component of the CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2 electrode material after the stability test were investigated by SEM, XRD and XPS techniques (Fig. S11). The FE-SEM image of the used catalyst (Fig. S11a) clearly shows the presence of a mace-like structure despite some slight damage. This result reflects the lack of a significant change in surface morphology after long-term stability testing. Comparison with the original XRD pattern of CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2 shows that the characteristic peaks in the used CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2 electrode material have not changed (Fig. S11b). The XPS spectra of the used CoNi-ZIF@NCNT-2 were deconvoluted into high-resolution Co $2p$ and Ni $2p$ XPS spectra (Figs. S11c and S11d). The Co $2p$ and Ni $2p$ peaks have shifted to higher binding energy relative to those in the spectraof the original catalyst, hinting that the electronic states of the Co and Ni sites in CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2 have changed after the stability test. This result indicates that Co and Ni sites are involved in electrochemical reactions, and can account for the excellent catalytic performance of the CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2 composite [84]. The above results reveal the potential of the CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2 electrode for long-term practical applications. Moreover, the practical application of the CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2//AC ASC device was verified by LED-lightened result with two symmetric devices (Fig. 4i).
The energy and power densities of the CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2//AC ASC were calculated with Eqs. (3) and (4). An energy density of 51.1 W h $\log^{-1}$ can be achieved at the power density of 860.1 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ , and the highest power density of 8500 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ can be obtained at the energy density of $32.1~\mathrm{Wh}~\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ . These values are considerably higher than the values in many similar reports (Fig. 5), such as CoNi-MOF/CFP//AC (28.5 Wh $\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ , 1500 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ ) [85], NiCo-MOF//AC (20.9 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ , 800 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ ) [86], ZNC LDH67//AC (27.9 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ , 1300 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ ) [87], ${\mathsf{N i C o-L D H@N C F//A C}}$ C (25.7 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ , 1500 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ ) [88], $\mathsf{N i-C o P@C@C N T//r G O}$ (17.42 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ , 699 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ ) [69], and $\mathrm{CNT}@\mathrm{NiCo-LDH//CNT}@\mathrm{NC}$ (37.4 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ , $750~\mathrm{W~kg^{-1}}\mathrm{.}$ [89]. The above results confirm that the as-prepared CoNi-ZIF $@N$ -CNT-2 composite is suitable for SC electrode material.
## 4. Conclusion
A facile two-step approach was developed for the construction of a series of 1D/2D heterostructured CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT composites through the in situ growth of CoNi-ZIF NSs around $N\mathrm{.}$ -CNTs. The SC capability of the manufactured CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT composites can be efficiently modulated by the usage of metal precursors during the preparation of CoNi-ZIF NSs. CoNi-ZIF $@$ N-CNT-2 was composed of multiple functional components, such as CoO and $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ , that were accompanied by a mesoporous structure and the suitable coupling of the ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}/{\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ and $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}/\mathsf{N i}^{3+}$ redox species. Electrochemical tests revealed that the as-synthesized CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2 electrode displayed a high specific capacitance of $1118\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ as a benefit of its unique structure and outperformed other reported MOF-based electrodes. The SC performance of the present CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2 electrode is superior to those of MOF-related SCs [88]. In addition, the CoNi-ZIF@N-CNT-2-based ASC achieved a high energy density of 51.1 Wh $\displaystyle{\log^{-1}}$ at the power density of $860.1~\mathrm{W~kg}^{-1}$ , which are considerably higher than the values in many similar reports [85]. This work provides a facile and efficient route for the design and acquisition of MOF-based electrode materials with heterogeneous structures.
